Two per-draw costs on the drawing (FIFO) thread, found with a line-level profile of the title demo's busy part
(3,000-4,000 draws a frame) on a CPU held at its base clock:

1. upload_vpull: strips and fans with 16-bit indices took the general index loop, which tests the index width, the
   restart index and the strip prefix again for every index, and then the general strip walk. Now the prefix (at
   most two entries) goes through the general code and the batch's own indices through a tight loop that counts
   restarts; with no restart in the batch the strip walk is a plain loop. Same indices, range, triangles and strip
   state as before. VPULL_FAST16=0 turns both off together with the older 16-bit fast path.
2. vp_record_cb read back from the constant upload heap, which is write-combined memory and slow to read: c[0] right
   after the 7.5 KB copy, and the viewport epilogue changed in place. c[0] now comes from the rsx_state the constants
   were copied from, and the epilogue is built in a local array and copied once.

Title demo, CPU at base clock, two drawing threads: 28.3 flips/s before, 28.7 and 29.0 after (two runs); drawing
20.5 -> 19.2 ms a frame. Picture unchanged.
